WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.572
<v Examiner>How hearing occurs.

2
00:00:02.572 --> 00:00:03.245
<v Examiner>Four marks.

3
00:00:03.695 --> 00:00:08.376
<v Examiner>Discuss how hearing occurs in humans, from the sound wave arriving at the ear,

4
00:00:08.376 --> 00:00:10.116
<v Examiner>to the sound being perceived.

5
00:00:10.566 --> 00:00:11.693
This one is a chain.

6
00:00:11.693 --> 00:00:14.342
Get the links in order, and the marks fall out.

7
00:00:14.792 --> 00:00:17.928
The hook: collect, amplify, ripple, bend, fire, hear.

8
00:00:18.378 --> 00:00:18.884
Collect.

9
00:00:18.884 --> 00:00:24.263
In the outer ear, the pinna collects sound waves and channels them along the external

10
00:00:24.263 --> 00:00:27.807
auditory canal to the tympanic membrane, which vibrates.

11
00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:28.814
Amplify.

12
00:00:28.814 --> 00:00:35.080
In the middle ear, the vibration passes to the ossicles: malleus, then incus, then stapes.

13
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.561
They amplify it onto the much smaller oval window.

14
00:00:39.011 --> 00:00:39.462
Ripple.

15
00:00:39.462 --> 00:00:45.058
In the inner ear, the oval window vibrates the perilymph in the scala vestibuli and the

16
00:00:45.058 --> 00:00:50.461
scala tympani, and that passes the vibration on to the endolymph in the scala media,

17
00:00:50.461 --> 00:00:51.619
the cochlear duct.

18
00:00:53.069 --> 00:00:53.381
Bend.

19
00:00:53.381 --> 00:00:55.061
The basilar membrane moves,

20
00:00:55.061 --> 00:00:59.853
and the hair cells of the organ of Corti bend against the tectorial membrane.

21
00:01:00.303 --> 00:01:00.625
Fire.

22
00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:02.813
Bending the hairs is transduction.

23
00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:08.282
The hair cells are mechanoreceptors, and they turn the vibration into nerve impulses.

24
00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:09.038
Hear.

25
00:01:09.038 --> 00:01:13.746
The impulses travel along the cochlear branch of the vestibulocochlear nerve,

26
00:01:13.746 --> 00:01:17.721
cranial nerve eight, to the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe,

27
00:01:17.721 --> 00:01:19.249
where the sound is heard.

28
00:01:20.699 --> 00:01:21.316
The traps.

29
00:01:21.316 --> 00:01:24.335
It's the middle ear that amplifies: the ossicles.

30
00:01:24.335 --> 00:01:27.848
Her student-marked feedback slips and says the inner ear.

31
00:01:27.848 --> 00:01:30.683
Her deck says the middle ear, so write middle.

32
00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:33.826
Second: the hair cells sit on the basilar membrane,

33
00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:36.292
and bend against the tectorial membrane.

34
00:01:36.292 --> 00:01:39.435
Third: the auditory cortex is in the temporal lobe.

35
00:01:39.435 --> 00:01:41.222
The occipital lobe is vision.

36
00:01:41.672 --> 00:01:42.248
<v Examiner>Once more.

37
00:01:42.248 --> 00:01:43.400
<v Examiner>How hearing happens.

38
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.384
The pinna collects sound down the canal, and the tympanic membrane vibrates.

39
00:01:50.384 --> 00:01:54.738
The ossicles, malleus, incus and stapes, amplify it onto the oval window.

40
00:01:54.738 --> 00:01:59.331
That moves the perilymph, then the endolymph, and the basilar membrane moves,

41
00:01:59.331 --> 00:02:03.865
so the hair cells of the organ of Corti bend against the tectorial membrane.

42
00:02:03.865 --> 00:02:06.251
That's transduction into nerve impulses,

43
00:02:06.251 --> 00:02:10.725
which travel along nerve eight to the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.
